[Black "Nguyen Van Huy vs. Le Quang Liem"]
[Result "0-1"]

 { Nguyen Van Huy – Le Quang Liem, Ho Chi Minh City 2012 Learning objective: This game illustrates Black’s minority attack. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. g3 d5 4. Bg2 b5 5. O-O Nbd7 6. Bg5 Be7 7. Nbd2 c5 8. dxc5 Nxc5 { After White’s unambitious opening, we have a Slav formation with reversed colours. White needs a central break to avoid being inferior. } 9. Nd4 Bd7 10. c3 O-O 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. e4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Bxe4 Rb8 $15 { We have reached the structure of interest. Black is doing well for two reasons: 1. He has the pair of bishops in an open position. 2. His b5-pawn is ready for a minority attack; White will be unable to expand his queenside majority without it being weakened. } 15. Qd3 ( { Eliminating Black’s bishop pair with } 15. Nc6 $2 Bxc6 16. Bxc6 { loses a pawn after } b4 $1 17. Qxd8 Rfxd8 18. cxb4 Rxb4 $17 { when the b2-pawn is lost. } ) 
h6 16. Rad1 ( { Of course } 16. b3 $2 { (to create a passed pawn with c3-c4) is simply met by } Qc7 17. c4 bxc4 18. bxc4 Rfc8 $17 { when the c4-pawn is a weakness and not a strength. } ) 
Qc7 17. a3 Rfc8 18. Qf3 { Without being able to expand, White can only run on the spot, waiting for Black to act. } ( { Attempting kingside action with } 18. f4 a5 19. f5 e5 { does not help White, and after } 20. Nc2 Bc6 $5 21. Ne3 b4 $1 22. Bxc6 Qxc6 23. axb4 axb4 24. c4 Bd8 $1 { ⩱ Black will follow up with ...Bb6 and White’s king will become vulnerable. } ) 
a5 19. Rd3 $2 { White should have fought harder to avoid the ...b5-b4 break. } ( { Better was } 19. Nc2 Be8 20. Qe2 Be7 $15 ( { or } 20. Rd3 Rd8 $5 ) 
 { (though the ...b5-b4 break will come sooner or later.) } ) 
Be8 20. Rfd1 b4 $1 { The minority attack has been completed; White will be left with a weak pawn on the queenside. } 21. axb4 axb4 22. cxb4 Rxb4 23. Rc3 ( { If } 23. b3 Qb6 24. Ne2 Bg5 $5 { ∓ White must deal with the weak b3-pawn as well as his misplaced pieces; one threat is ...f5 . } ) 
Qb8 24. Nc6 Bxc6 25. Rxc6 Rxc6 26. Bxc6 Rxb2 $17 { The minority attack has succeeded, Black has gained a pawn and now he must try to win the 4 vs. 3 battle on the kingside. } 27. Ra1 $2 { Did White want to trade rooks and queens even at the cost of a second pawn? } ( { Better was } 27. Rd7 $1 Qe5 28. Kg2 g6 $17 { though Black may make progress slowly. } ) 
Rxf2 28. Ra8 Qxa8 29. Bxa8 Rxf3 30. Bxf3 { We have an opposite-coloured bishop endgame, but as it turns out, two connected pawns are often enough to win such an endgame as long as there are more pawns on the board. } Bd4+ ( { PH: This is a mistake, } 30... g6 { immediately is necessary. If White tries the same plan as we'll see later at move 32 with } 31. g4 $2 { , then after } Bh4 { White will lose: } 32. Kf1 f5 33. gxf5 gxf5 34. Bd1 Kf7 35. Ke2 Kf6 36. Kf3 e5 37. Bc2 Kg5 38. Bd3 { and now } e4 39. Bxe4 fxe4 40. Kxe4 Kg4 { Black is left with a wrong colored bishop, but White won't get to the h1-square so Black wins anyway. } ) 
31. Kg2 g6 32. Bd1 ( { PH: } 32. g4 $1 f5 33. h4 $1 { followed by h5 is a relatively trivial draw. } ) 
f5 33. h4 ( { Another try was: } 33. g4 Kf7 34. gxf5 gxf5 35. Kf3 e5 36. Bc2 { Attempting to build a fortress and sacrifice the bishop for two pawns when needed: } Kf6 ( { Of course not } 36. h4 e4 $2 37. Bxe4 fxe4 38. Kxe4 { which is a theoretical draw, because the bishop is on squares of the wrong colour. } ) 
37. Bd3 Kg5 38. Bc2 Ba7 $1 39. h4 ( { Or } 39. Bb1 e4 $1 40. Bxe4 fxe4 41. Kxe4 Kg4 { and Black wins. } ) 
Kg6 40. Bb1 Bb6 41. Bc2 Bd8 $1 { This is the key. } 42. h5 Kg5 43. Bb1 Bb6 44. Bc2 e4 45. Bxe4 fxe4 46. Kxe4 Kg4 $1 ( { But not } 46. h4 Kxh5 $4 47. Kf3 { and draws. } ) 
47. Kd3 Kxh5 48. Ke2 Kg4 49. Kf1 Kf3 { And Black wins. } ) 
h5 34. Ba4 Kf7 35. Kf3 Bb6 36. Bb5 Ba5 37. Bc6 Be1 38. Kf4 $4 e5+ 39. Kf3 ( { Or } 39. Kxe5 Bxg3 40. Kd5 Bxh4 { and wins. } ) 
g5 $1 { Now Black wraps up the game very nicely. } 40. hxg5 Kg6 41. Kg2 Kxg5 42. Kh3 ( { Also hopeless is } 42. Bd5 e4 $1 43. Bc6 h4 44. gxh4 Bxh4 { . } ) 
h4 43. gxh4+ Bxh4 { Even though there are only two pawns left, Black is winning because White does not have enough time to set up the standard defence with Kf3 and Bd3. } 44. Kg2 e4 45. Kf1 Kf6 46. Ke2 Ke5 47. Be8 f4 ( { White resigns. } 47... f4 47. Bh5 { is inadequate as } Kf5 $1 { places White in zugzwang: } 49. Kd2 f3 50. Ke3 Bg5 51. Kf2 Kf4 $19 { Final remarks 1. Black’s bishop pair proved very useful, as White had no entry points on the d-file and could not create counterplay with it. 2. A red flag for White should have been the b5-pawn, which hinted that a queenside expansion would not be achievable hence White should stay passive. 3. White’s biggest problem in this game was his rather questionable opening, which left him fighting for equality from the very beginning. Black’s play was impeccable. } ) 

 0-1

